      "why": "Full pipeline gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax and it can meet the 32 Msps constraint at a modest Fmax. It is the natural area/accuracy trade-off family: area scales with N and W, accuracy with N and guards. I focus W and N in the 14..20 band because the 2^-12 error bound is the binding accuracy constraint and W=14..16 with N~14..18 is where trunc/round and small guards can hit it at minimum area; I keep angle_guard/frac_guard low to save area since accuracy is dominated by N and W."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 2,
          "low": -1,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 20,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 20,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, cutting flip-flops (and area) versus the full pipeline while still giving 1 result/cycle, so it can meet the 32 Msps throughput constraint at lower area. The cost is higher latency (ceil(N/m)+2 cycles), which must stay within the 0.5 us p99 batch window at the achievable Fmax. I search m=2..4 to find the area/latency sweet spot: larger m saves registers but risks the batch deadline, so the front should show where that trade-off breaks. Same W/N/guard band as pipelined since accuracy is identical across families."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Both allowed families produce 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax and the 32 Msps constraint reduces to needing Fmax >= 32 MHz, which is easily met; the real trade-offs are area vs accuracy (W, N, guards, rounding) and, for pipelined_m, area vs latency (m). The binding accuracy constraint is max_abs_err <= 2^-12, which is set by N and W (guards/rounding are secondary), so I concentrate W and N in 14..20 and keep guards low to minimise area. I split the budget 55/45 between pipelined (the baseline full pipeline, likely to anchor the accuracy end of the front) and pipelined_m (the area-reduction variant, likely to anchor the low-area end), so the merged front covers the whole area/accuracy curve. The p99 batch deadline (0.5 us for 32 results) is the main risk for pipelined_m's higher latency, so I keep m modest (2..4) and let the L1 bound/L2 sim screen out designs that miss it."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The Pareto front is well-established with 7 feasible designs spanning area 39973-59014 um^2 and accuracy 12.4-15.6 bits. The minimum area design (pipelined, data_width=19, n_iter=14, angle_guard=-1, frac_guard=0, rounding=round) achieves 39973 um^2 with 12.4 bits accuracy (max_abs_err=0.000181 < 0.000244141 constraint) and 138 MSPS throughput (>> 32 MSPS constraint). The sys_p99_batch_us constraint is met by the best designs (0.28 us < 0.5 us). The pipelined family dominates the front; pipelined_m yielded 0 feasible designs. Since this is the final round, the current front adequately maps the trade-off space for the selection rule (min area), which picks the 39973 um^2 design."
}
